    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> rulers such as Al-Mu'tamid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1085 and pressed on the other taifas.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> kept an elite reserve hidden behind a mound and attacked the Castilian camp while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso</a>'s troops were pursuing the Andalusian line, and Alfonso broke off to save his camp.</li><li>Within about eighteen months of the battle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> had conquered the major cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and ended the era of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kings.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Muslim army is said to have brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> whose sight and sound frightened the Castilian horses, and Arabic sources call the field az-Zallaqah, 'slippery ground', after the bloodshed there.</p></aside>
    </section>

