    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1082</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutamid-ibn-abbad">Al-Mu'tamid ibn Abbad</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> writes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> asking for help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1085</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> captures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, which Muslims had held for 372 years, and takes lands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1086</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> as his base and crosses into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> with his army</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1086</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> proposes fighting on a Monday, and the armies camp either side of the Wadi Bira</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1086</span><span class="rf-text">The Muslim army defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> at Sagrajas, and he escapes to Coria after dark</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1088</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> returns to besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aledo">Aledo</a> and withdraws after four months</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1090</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> crosses a third time and besieges <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, then lifts the siege for lack of supplies</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1091</span><span class="rf-text">Almoravid armies capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> falls, ending the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kings</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-sagrajas" data-v="144f0bc4c0" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies face each other across the river</b> The two armies had camped facing each other with only the Wadi Bira between them. The Muslims put heavy cavalry in front and kept the Almoravid reserve hidden behind a high mound; Alfonso drew up two divisions and a vanguard.</li><li><b>The Castilian first division is thrown back</b> Alfonso sent his first division against Al-Mu'tamid, but it met the Almoravid cavalry before reaching him. The Almoravid archers helped drive it back to its second line, though the Almoravids lost men.</li><li><b>Alfonso attacks and Sir's battalion is sent</b> Alfonso then attacked the Almoravids with Al-Mu'tamid. Dawud ibn Aisha sent word to Yusuf ibn Tashfin, who dispatched a battalion that broke into the Castilian army and linked up with Al-Mu'tamid's forces.</li><li><b>Alfonso reaches the tents and the vanguard gives way</b> Alfonso kept up the pressure until he reached the Almoravid tents and breached the trench round them. Al-Mu'tamid's Andalusian forces were driven back, and the Castilians went after them.</li><li><b>The reserve strikes the Castilian camp</b> While the Castilians chased Al-Mu'tamid, Yusuf ibn Tashfin brought up his reserve and attacked their camp. The Castilian horses panicked at the camels, which he set ablaze, and the camp's remaining men fled towards Alfonso.</li><li><b>Alfonso turns back and the Muslims counter-attack</b> Alfonso gave up the pursuit and withdrew to save his camp. Al-Mu'tamid ordered an attack, and Sir ibn Abi Bakr's men charged Alfonso's forces as they fell back.</li><li><b>The Takrur cavalry charge and Alfonso leaves the field</b> Yusuf ibn Tashfin ordered 4,000 black cavalry from Takrur to charge. Their charge broke the Christian cavalry, and Alfonso retreated with a small contingent to a nearby hill and escaped after dark.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fitna-of-al-andalus">Fitna of al-Andalus</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> broke up into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms. The most powerful was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbadid-dynasty">Banu Abbad</a>; others included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> under the Dhulnunid dynasty, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hud">Banu Hud</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aftasid-dynasty">Banu al-Aftas</a>. Their rulers were often more hostile to one another than to their Christian neighbours, and many bought military support from the northern kingdoms with tribute. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutamid-ibn-abbad">Al-Mu'tamid ibn Abbad</a> of Seville allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a> and paid him tribute in return for help against other taifa rulers.</p><p>In 1085 Alfonso VI captured Toledo, which Muslims had held for 372 years, and made it his capital. He also took the lands along both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>, including the fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, Mérida and Badajoz, although Al-Mu'tamid warned him that this broke their agreement. Alfonso, with the intention of subjugating all the Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a>, pressed on, and his forces besieged Zaragoza. Most Muslim rulers concluded that only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> could help them.</p><p>The Almoravids were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> reform movement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> tribes. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> they controlled the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> from their capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>. Al-Mutawakkil ibn al-Aftas of Badajoz and Al-Mu'tamid, who wrote in 1082, both appealed to him. Arabic sources add that Al-Mu'tamid had Alfonso's tax collector, Ibn Shalib, killed after an insult, and that a gathering at Córdoba then agreed to ask Yusuf for help. Yusuf's price was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>, which Al-Mu'tamid gave up.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Muslim side was in two camps, the Andalusian and the Almoravid. Al-Mu'tamid was overall commander of the Andalusian forces, which included troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> under Ibn Sumadih, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> under Abdallah ibn Buluggin with his brother Tamim of Málaga, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> under Al-Mutawakkil ibn al-Aftas. Al-Mu'tamid led the vanguard with Dawud ibn Aisha and ten thousand Almoravid cavalry. Al-Mutawakkil held the right flank, troops of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> the left, and the rear was the other Andalusian forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> kept the reserve, an elite group of Almoravid fighters from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and his personal guard.</p><p>The 4,000 black soldiers in the battle are called hasham, paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khallikan">Ibn Khallikan</a>, which suggests they were not slaves. According to Ibn 'Idhari, Yusuf had bought black slaves in 1071-1072 and added about 2,000 of them to his cavalry. Umar al-Naqar suggests they may have come from the Muslim kingdom of Takrur. Yusuf also had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> brought from Morocco, intended to unsettle the Frankish cavalry.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> had volunteers from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as well as men from Aragon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a>. His army is put at 60,000. He divided it in two. Count García and Count Zodric led the first division, to attack the Muslim vanguard. The second had two wings under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ramirez">Sancho Ramírez</a> of Aragon and Count Raymond, with Alfonso at the centre and Albaráns commanding the vanguard. The armies faced each other across the Wadi Bira.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso</a> arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajab">Rajab</a> 479 AH (October 1086) and, on a Thursday, proposed battle for the Monday. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf</a>'s letter to Al-Mu'izz ibn Badis, he gave as his reason that Friday was the Muslim holy day, Saturday the Jewish and Sunday the Christian one. The Muslims suspected treachery and set scouts to watch. On the morning of Friday 23 October (12 Rajab) they reported that Alfonso was mobilising, and Al-Mu'tamid called on Yusuf for help.</p><p>Alfonso sent García and Zodric to strike Al-Mu'tamid's camp. They met the Almoravid cavalry of Dawud ibn Aisha first, who with many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> drove back the first attack but took losses. The front line of Al-Mu'tamid's Andalusians then gave way, and some Andalusian leaders fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a>, while Al-Mu'tamid and the knights of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> held on, surrounded. Alfonso next attacked Ibn Aisha's men, reached the Almoravid tents and crossed the trench in front of them. Yusuf had sent a battalion under Sir ibn Abi Bakr, which broke into the Castilian army and joined Al-Mu'tamid.</p><p>While the Castilians chased Al-Mu'tamid, Yusuf rode with his reserve against the Castilian camp, where horses panicked at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, and he set the camels on fire. Alfonso broke off the pursuit to save his camp. Al-Mu'tamid's men and those who had fled to Badajoz then attacked, and 4,000 black cavalry from Takrur charged with lamta shields and javelins. One of them wounded Alfonso severely in the thigh, which left him with a limp. At sunset Alfonso withdrew to a hill and fled in the dark to Coria.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso</a>, who had arrived with an army of 60,000, retreated to a mountain fortress with around 300 knights. Spanish historians stress that his defeat was catastrophic and that he escaped in a pitiful state with a few companions. The sieges of the major Andalusian cities were lifted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf</a> ordered a proclamation of the victory to be read in mosques across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, and Al-Mu'tamid told his son in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>.</p><p>Yusuf stayed near Seville for three days and then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> because his son and heir Abu Bakr had died, and he feared instability in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. Before leaving, he urged the Andalusian rulers to unite, according to Ibn Buluggin, and left 3,000 Almoravid soldiers with Al-Mu'tamid. Sir ibn Abi Bakr, appointed to lead the Almoravid forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, went with Al-Mutawakkil into central Portugal as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>. Al-Mu'tamid took several fortresses near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> but was pushed back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a>'s knights.</p><p>The rulers fell back into disputes, and the Castilians resumed raiding from the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aledo">Aledo</a>. Yusuf returned in 1088 to besiege it, withdrew after four months, and returned again in 1090. Over the following years the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> deposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kings. Abdallah ibn Buluggin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and then Al-Mu'tamid were captured and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghmat">Aghmat</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known mainly from Arabic sources and later Muslim historians. They preserve letters between the Andalusian rulers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>, and the words of Ibn Buluggin, Ibn al-Kardabus and the poet al-Hamiri. Yusuf's letter to Al-Mu'izz ibn Badis is the source for the agreement to fight on Monday and for Alfonso's supposed plan to attack early. Joseph Aschbach, a German historian, wrote that Alfonso thought he might use deceit in war and break a sworn pact.</p><p>The names differ with the tradition. Muslim sources call it the Battle of az-Zallaqah, from the plain, and the Arabic name is said to mean slippery ground. In European accounts the first phase, against Al-Mu'tamid and Dawud ibn Aisha, is the Battle of Rueda and the second, against Yusuf, the Battle of Sacralias. Historians also disagree about the black troops, whether paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> or slaves and from where.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muslim writers treated the battle as a great deliverance. Muhammad ibn al-Samak al-Amili wrote that it was a day unmatched since Yarmouk and Qadisiyyah. Ibn al-Kardabus said the stranglehold on the peninsula was eased, and Ibn Buluggin that since that day the Christians had been filled with fear. Before the battle, Andalusian rulers had been paying tribute to their Christian neighbours, and afterwards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf</a> was hailed as a saviour and his name was invoked in mosques. His influence grew across both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>.</p><p>Spanish historians stress the scale of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso</a>'s defeat, though he went on launching new offensives and never again had the same success. The battle halted Christian expansion for a time and preserved al-Andalus from Alfonso's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> efforts.</p><p>The political result ran against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kings who had called for help. The rulers returned to their quarrels, Yusuf crossed again in 1088 and 1090, and within eighteen months the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, Málaga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>. That ended the era of the taifa kings and began Almoravid control of al-Andalus.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Sagrajas, or az-Zallaqah, on 23 October 1086 Almoravid and Andalusian forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> and Al-Mu'tamid defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a>, who fled by night to Coria. The victory halted his advance but was followed by Almoravid conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms.</em></p>

